{"kind":"request","text":"Build the GitHub connector's outbound half: a fix for an observed issue becomes a pull request, so approval and merge happen the usual way. Second half of hugh's 2026-08-10 framing; rests on the inbound half being in place.\n\nThe design separates two outbound things and this request follows that split. Rendering is continuous and idempotent — a projection onto a surface the connector owns, stamped with the frontier it rendered — and deserves no ceremony. Opening a pull request is a discrete side effect, and discrete side effects go through the work loop like any other work: the connector is requested, promises, acts on GitHub, and reports with the resulting object as evidence. Delivery failure then shows up in the room as an unkept promise rather than a silent error in a log nobody reads.\n\nThe point of doing it this way, rather than having the connector merge things itself, is that the existing exact-head discipline still decides what lands. gitseq's own review and merge guard stay in charge; GitHub carries the conversation and the button. A pull request is a rendering surface, not an authority.\n\nWorth stating plainly because it is the interesting half of the dogfood: the connector must never read back its own writing. It owns the comment it edits in place and ingests only what it does not own. Suppression by tagging is fragile; suppression by structure is not.","body":{"conditions":"Work that fixes an observed issue can be pushed as a branch and opened as a pull request that names the originating issue and the governing gitseq request; opening the pull request goes through the work loop as a command — requested, promised, and reported with the created pull request as evidence — rather than happening as a silent side effect, so a delivery failure appears as an unkept promise; the pull request body carries the exact head under review and the artifact, so a reader on GitHub can find the durable record; gitseq's own review and merge guard remain what decides whether the head lands, and the connector neither approves nor merges; the connector edits only surfaces it owns and ingests nothing it wrote, demonstrated by a test that renders and then re-observes without producing a new act; the flow is exercised end to end on a real gitseq issue as the dogfood case; `make test` and `go vet` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}